# Solution 17.3 — a game with a draw in it.
#
# `p` and `q` move to each other forever, so neither player can force
# anything there. `c` wins by moving to a dead end. The Sprague-Grundy
# constraints are gone: they are facts about Nim, not about games.
#
# Running this reports the determinacy constraint as violated, and the rows it
# reports are exactly the drawn positions:
#
#   Constraint `!- not_lost^(P), not win(P).` is violated by 2 rows:
#     P = "p"
#     P = "q"
#
# Which of the two to show a user depends on the question. `win` is the safe
# one for "can I force a win from here"; `not_lost^` answers "is it still worth
# playing". Showing the gap between them is better than either.

move("p", "q"). move("q", "p"). move("c", "dead").

output predicate win(P)       :- move(P, Q), not not_lost^(Q).
output predicate not_lost^(P) :- move(P, Q), not win(Q).

output predicate drawn(P) :- not_lost^(P), not win(P).

!- not_lost^(P), not win(P).
